---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Methodology"
  description="How PeptiBloom approaches scientific evidence, uncertainty, provenance, contradictions and the separation between scientific and commercial information."
>
  <section class="page-hero section">
    <div class="site-frame page-hero__grid">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Methodology</p>
        <h1>Evidence is useful only when its limits remain visible.</h1>
      </div>
      <p class="page-hero__lead">
        PeptiBloom does not treat all information as equivalent. The public library is designed to preserve
        provenance, distinguish evidence from interpretation and keep uncertainty visible.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <article class="section methodology-article">
    <div class="site-frame">
      <div class="reading-column prose">
        <section aria-labelledby="principles-title">
          <p class="eyebrow">Publication principles</p>
          <h2 id="principles-title">What the library is trying to protect.</h2>
          <ul class="statement-list">
            <li>Clearly distinguish what is known, unknown, theoretical, contradictory and anecdotal.</li>
            <li>Do not present opinion as fact.</li>
            <li>Do not create scientific claims, studies, compounds or evidence to fill UI.</li>
            <li>Keep evidence provenance inspectable.</li>
            <li>Publish production scientific content only after it completes the approved review process.</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <section aria-labelledby="process-title">
          <p class="eyebrow">Editorial process</p>
          <h2 id="process-title">Source → review → interpretation → traceability.</h2>
          <p>
            The sequence is deliberately simple at the public-web level. It describes an editorial expectation,
            not the scientific data model: begin with inspectable sources, review what they actually support,
            interpret without manufacturing certainty, and preserve a route back to provenance and limitations.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section aria-labelledby="uncertainty-method-title">
          <p class="eyebrow">Uncertainty</p>
          <h2 id="uncertainty-method-title">Contradictions and evidence gaps are not editorial failures.</h2>
          <p>
            When evidence is incomplete, inconsistent or unresolved, the library should say so. PeptiBloom’s
            goal is not to force every question into a confident answer, but to make the current state of
            knowledge legible.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section aria-labelledby="provenance-title">
          <p class="eyebrow">Provenance</p>
          <h2 id="provenance-title">The path back to the source should remain inspectable.</h2>
          <p>
            Scientific statements should not become detached from the material that supports them. As the
            Evidence Layer matures, the public interface will expose production-safe source and review context
            through an approved contract rather than through hand-maintained scientific pages.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section aria-labelledby="boundary-title">
          <p class="eyebrow">Science &amp; commerce</p>
          <h2 id="boundary-title">Scientific evidence and commercial information remain structurally distinct.</h2>
          <p>
            Product, inventory, supplier and batch concepts belong to operational domains. The scientific
            library does not use those concepts as substitutes for evidence, and the web does not create
            relationships between scientific and commercial data unless an explicit cross-domain contract is approved.
          </p>
        </section>
      </div>
    </div>
  </article>

  <section class="section section--soft">
    <div class="site-frame reading-column">
      <p class="eyebrow">Transparency</p>
      <h2>This method applies to a PeptiBloom-owned scientific resource.</h2>
      <p>
        The Scientific Library is part of PeptiBloom. Its ownership should remain visible alongside the methods
        used to review and present scientific information.
      </p>
      <a class="editorial-link" href="/about/">About PeptiBloom and ownership <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
